(* CreatedBy='biomolecular-condensates build pipeline' *)

(* Beginning of Notebook Content *)
Notebook[{Cell["Biomolecular Condensates and the Origin of Life", "Title"], Cell["From Flory\[Dash]Huggins thermodynamics to protocellular compartments \[LongDash] first-principles mathematical modelling of liquid\[Dash]liquid phase separation in biology", "Subtitle"], Cell["Wolfram Community submission \[LongDash] June 2026", "Subsubtitle", FontSlant -> Italic, FontColor -> GrayLevel[0.4]], Cell["Abstract", "Section"], Cell[TextData[{"Biomolecular condensates are ", StyleBox["membrane-free liquid droplets", FontWeight -> Bold], " inside cells, formed by liquid\[Dash]liquid phase separation (LLPS) of proteins and nucleic acids. Discovered as P granules in ", StyleBox["C. elegans", FontSlant -> Italic], " germ cells (Brangwynne et al., 2009), they have since been found to organise virtually every aspect of cellular function: gene regulation, signal transduction, stress response, and the formation of structures from the nucleolus to synaptic densities."}], "Text", FontSize -> 14, CellMargins -> {{60, 60}, {6, 6}}, Background -> RGBColor[0.98, 0.96, 0.9]], Cell[TextData[{"This notebook develops the mathematical physics of condensates from first principles. We implement the ", StyleBox["Flory\[Dash]Huggins", FontWeight -> Bold], " free energy to derive the phase diagram, solve the ", StyleBox["Cahn\[Dash]Hilliard", FontWeight -> Bold], " partial differential equation for spinodal decomposition, compute the ", StyleBox["concentration enhancement", FontWeight -> Bold], " that makes condensates natural chemical reactors, and model the ", StyleBox["protocell dynamics", FontWeight -> Bold], " (growth, division, competition) that connect condensates to the origin of life. We conclude with the ", StyleBox["liquid \[Rule] gel \[Rule] aggregate", FontWeight -> Bold], " transition that links condensate dysfunction to neurodegenerative disease."}], "Text", FontSize -> 14, CellMargins -> {{60, 60}, {6, 6}}, Background -> RGBColor[0.98, 0.96, 0.9]], Cell[TextData[{StyleBox["Audience:", FontSlant -> Italic], " the prose is written for two overlapping audiences. Each section opens with an intuitive explanation that requires no background, then deepens into a quantitative treatment with equations, numerical integration, and visualisations. Biophysicists will recognise the canonical references; readers from other backgrounds should be able to follow the physical narrative."}], "Text", FontSize -> 14, CellMargins -> {{60, 60}, {6, 6}}, Background -> RGBColor[0.98, 0.96, 0.9]], Cell["1.  The membrane-free organelle", "Section"], Cell[TextData[{"For over a century, cell biology was built on the assumption that the cell's internal organisation depends on ", StyleBox["membranes", FontWeight -> Bold], ": lipid bilayers that enclose the nucleus, the mitochondria, the endoplasmic reticulum. Compartmentalisation by membranes was the organising principle of eukaryotic life."}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell[TextData[{"Then, in 2009, ", StyleBox["Brangwynne, Eckmann, Courson, Rybarska, Hoege, Gharakhani, J\[UDoubleDot]licher, and Hyman", FontWeight -> Bold], " showed that P granules \[LongDash] RNA\[Dash]protein bodies in the germ cells of the nematode ", StyleBox["C. elegans", FontSlant -> Italic], " \[LongDash] are not membrane-bound organelles at all. They are ", StyleBox["liquid droplets", FontWeight -> Bold], ". They flow, they fuse, they drip, they dissolve and re-form. They are phase-separated condensates of RNA and proteins, held together not by a lipid membrane but by the same physics that separates oil from water."}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell[TextData[{"This discovery launched a revolution. Within a decade, dozens of cellular structures were reclassified as condensates: stress granules, nucleoli, Cajal bodies, PML bodies, centrosomes, synaptic densities, heterochromatin domains, transcriptional hubs. The 2024 Nobel Prize in Chemistry was awarded to David Baker for protein design, but the intellectual context \[LongDash] that proteins are not just rigid machines but can phase-separate into functional liquids \[LongDash] owes as much to the condensate revolution."}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell[TextData[{StyleBox["The physics.", FontSlant -> Italic], " The formation of a condensate is a thermodynamic phase transition. Below a critical temperature (or above a critical interaction strength), a homogeneous solution of macromolecules becomes thermodynamically unstable and spontaneously separates into a dilute phase and a dense phase. The dense phase is the condensate. The transition is governed by a competition between the entropy of mixing (which favours homogeneity) and the enthalpic interactions between molecules (which favour like-with-like aggregation). The mathematical framework for this competition is ", StyleBox["Flory\[Dash]Huggins theory", FontWeight -> Bold], ", which we derive in the next section."}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell[TextData[{StyleBox["The origin-of-life connection.", FontSlant -> Italic], " Perhaps the most provocative implication of the condensate revolution is its connection to the ", StyleBox["origin of life", FontWeight -> Bold], ". Aleksandr Oparin proposed in 1924 that life began inside ", StyleBox["coacervate droplets", FontWeight -> Bold], " \[LongDash] spontaneously forming colloidal structures that concentrate organic molecules from the dilute primordial ocean. Oparin's hypothesis was dismissed for decades because the known coacervates were too disordered to sustain biochemistry. But modern condensates, with their exquisite sequence-dependent phase behaviour and their ability to concentrate specific enzymes by factors of 100\[Dash]1000, revive Oparin's vision in molecular detail. We show in \[Section]4 that condensate-assisted chemistry solves the ", StyleBox["concentration problem", FontWeight -> Bold], " that has bedevilled prebiotic chemistry: how do you get reactions going in a picomolar ocean?"}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell["2.  Thermodynamics of phase separation", "Section"], Cell["2.1  The Flory\[Dash]Huggins free energy", "Subsection"], Cell[TextData[{StyleBox["The lattice model.", FontSlant -> Italic], " Consider a lattice of ", StyleBox["N\:209c", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], " sites, each occupied by either a monomer of species 1 (the polymer, chain length ", StyleBox["N\:2081", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], ") or a monomer of species 2 (the solvent, with ", StyleBox["N\:2082 = 1", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], "). Let ", StyleBox["\[Phi]", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], " be the volume fraction of species 1. The Flory\[Dash]Huggins free energy of mixing per lattice site is:"}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell[BoxData[FormBox[RowBox[{FractionBox["f", "kT"], "=", FractionBox[RowBox[{"\[Phi]", " ", RowBox[{"ln", " ", "\[Phi]"}]}], SubscriptBox["N", "1"]], "+", FractionBox[RowBox[{"(", RowBox[{"1", "-", "\[Phi]"}], ")", " ", RowBox[{"ln", "(", RowBox[{"1", "-", "\[Phi]"}], ")"}]}], SubscriptBox["N", "2"]], "+", RowBox[{"\[Chi]", " ", "\[Phi]", "(", RowBox[{"1", "-", "\[Phi]"}], ")"}]}], TraditionalForm]], "DisplayFormula", CellMargins -> {{80, 50}, {8, 8}}], Cell[TextData[{"The first two terms are the ", StyleBox["entropy of mixing", FontWeight -> Bold], " \[LongDash] always negative (favour mixing) and scaled by the inverse chain lengths ", StyleBox["1/N\:2081", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], " and ", StyleBox["1/N\:2082", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], ". Longer chains have less translational entropy per monomer, so polymers phase-separate more readily than small molecules. The third term is the ", StyleBox["enthalpy of mixing", FontWeight -> Bold], ", parameterised by the Flory interaction parameter ", StyleBox["\[Chi]", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], ". For most condensate-forming proteins, \[Chi] > 0 (unfavourable mixing), and \[Chi] decreases with temperature (\[Chi] \[Proportional] 1/T), giving an upper critical solution temperature (UCST) phase diagram."}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell[BoxData[GraphicsBox[TagBox[RasterBox[CompressedData["1:"], {{0, 0}, {520, 286}}, {0, 255}, ColorFunction -> RGBColor],BoxForm`ImageTag["Byte", ColorSpace -> "RGB", Interleaving -> True], Selectable -> False], DefaultBaseStyle -> "ImageGraphics", ImageSize -> 640, ImageSizeRaw -> {520, 286}, PlotRange -> {{0, 520}, {0, 286}}]], "Output", TextAlignment -> Center, CellMargins -> {{Automatic, Automatic}, {10, 6}}], Cell[TextData[{"Figure 2.1  Flory\[Dash]Huggins free energy density for a polymer\[Dash]solvent system (N\:2081 = 100, N\:2082 = 1) at several values of \[Chi]. Below the critical \[Chi], the free energy has a single minimum (stable mixed state). Above it, a double well appears: the system phase-separates into dilute (\[Phi]\:2097) and dense (\[Phi]\:1d48) phases at the two minima."}], "Text", FontSlant -> Italic, FontSize -> 11, FontColor -> GrayLevel[0.35], CellMargins -> {{70, 70}, {2, 14}}], Cell["2.2  Spinodal and binodal: the phase diagram", "Subsection"], Cell[TextData[{"The thermodynamic stability of the mixed state is determined by the curvature of the free energy. The ", StyleBox["spinodal", FontWeight -> Bold], " is the locus of inflection points:"}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell[BoxData[FormBox[RowBox[{FractionBox[RowBox[{SuperscriptBox["\[PartialD]", "2"], "f"}], RowBox[{"\[PartialD]", SuperscriptBox["\[Phi]", "2"]}]], "=", FractionBox["1", RowBox[{SubscriptBox["N", "1"], " ", "\[Phi]"}]], "+", FractionBox["1", RowBox[{SubscriptBox["N", "2"], " ", "(", RowBox[{"1", "-", "\[Phi]"}], ")"}]], "-", RowBox[{"2", "\[Chi]"}], "=", "0"}], TraditionalForm]], "DisplayFormula", CellMargins -> {{80, 50}, {8, 8}}], Cell[TextData[{"Inside the spinodal, the free energy is concave (", StyleBox["\[PartialD]\262f/\[PartialD]\[Phi]\262 < 0", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], ") and the system is ", StyleBox["unstable", FontWeight -> Bold], " to infinitesimal composition fluctuations \[LongDash] this is ", StyleBox["spinodal decomposition", FontWeight -> Bold], ". Between the spinodal and the ", StyleBox["binodal", FontWeight -> Bold], " (the coexistence curve), the system is ", StyleBox["metastable", FontWeight -> Bold], " \[LongDash] it requires a finite-amplitude fluctuation (nucleation) to phase-separate."}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell[TextData[{"The binodal is found by the ", StyleBox["common tangent construction", FontWeight -> Bold], ": the two coexisting compositions \[Phi]\:2097 and \[Phi]\:1d48 satisfy equal chemical potential and equal osmotic pressure:"}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell[BoxData[FormBox[RowBox[{RowBox[{"\[Mu]", "(", SubscriptBox["\[Phi]", "L"], ")"}], "=", RowBox[{"\[Mu]", "(", SubscriptBox["\[Phi]", "R"], ")"}], ",", "     ", RowBox[{"\[CapitalPi]", "(", SubscriptBox["\[Phi]", "L"], ")"}], "=", RowBox[{"\[CapitalPi]", "(", SubscriptBox["\[Phi]", "R"], ")"}]}], TraditionalForm]], "DisplayFormula", CellMargins -> {{80, 50}, {8, 8}}], Cell[TextData[{"The ", StyleBox["critical point", FontWeight -> Bold], " is where the spinodal and binodal meet:"}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell[BoxData[FormBox[RowBox[{SubscriptBox["\[Phi]", "c"], "=", FractionBox[SqrtBox[SubscriptBox["N", "2"]], RowBox[{SqrtBox[SubscriptBox["N", "1"]], "+", SqrtBox[SubscriptBox["N", "2"]]}]], ",", "     ", SubscriptBox["\[Chi]", "c"], "=", FractionBox[SuperscriptBox[RowBox[{"(", FractionBox["1", SqrtBox[SubscriptBox["N", "1"]]], "+", FractionBox["1", SqrtBox[SubscriptBox["N", "2"]]], ")"}], "2"], "2"]}], TraditionalForm]], "DisplayFormula", CellMargins -> {{80, 50}, {8, 8}}], Cell[TextData[{"For a long polymer (", StyleBox["N\:2081 = 100", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], ", ", StyleBox["N\:2082 = 1", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], "): ", StyleBox["\[Phi]\:1d9c = 0.091", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], ", ", StyleBox["\[Chi]\:1d9c = 0.605", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], ". The critical concentration is low (~9%), meaning that even dilute polymer solutions phase-separate when \[Chi] exceeds a modest threshold."}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell[BoxData[GraphicsBox[TagBox[RasterBox[CompressedData["1:"], {{0, 0}, {520, 292}}, {0, 255}, ColorFunction -> RGBColor],BoxForm`ImageTag["Byte", ColorSpace -> "RGB", Interleaving -> True], Selectable -> False], DefaultBaseStyle -> "ImageGraphics", ImageSize -> 640, ImageSizeRaw -> {520, 292}, PlotRange -> {{0, 520}, {0, 292}}]], "Output", TextAlignment -> Center, CellMargins -> {{Automatic, Automatic}, {10, 6}}], Cell[TextData[{"Figure 2.2  Phase diagram in \[Chi]\[Dash]\[Phi] coordinates. The blue curve is the binodal (coexistence); the red dashed curve is the spinodal (stability limit). Between them: metastable (nucleation and growth). Inside the spinodal: unstable (spinodal decomposition). The black dot is the critical point."}], "Text", FontSlant -> Italic, FontSize -> 11, FontColor -> GrayLevel[0.35], CellMargins -> {{70, 70}, {2, 14}}], Cell["2.3  Chain length and multivalency", "Subsection"], Cell[TextData[{"A key prediction of Flory\[Dash]Huggins theory is that ", StyleBox["longer polymers phase-separate more readily", FontWeight -> Bold], ". The critical \[Chi] decreases as ", StyleBox["1/\[Sqrt]N", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], ", meaning that a polymer of 1000 monomers needs only 1/10th the interaction strength of a 10-mer. This is biologically significant: intrinsically disordered regions (IDRs) of condensate-forming proteins are typically 50\[Dash]500 residues, and their effective ", StyleBox["N", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], " is further increased by multivalent interactions (multiple binding sites per molecule, RNA scaffolds, post-translational modifications)."}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell[BoxData[GraphicsBox[TagBox[RasterBox[CompressedData["1:"], {{0, 0}, {520, 302}}, {0, 255}, ColorFunction -> RGBColor],BoxForm`ImageTag["Byte", ColorSpace -> "RGB", Interleaving -> True], Selectable -> False], DefaultBaseStyle -> "ImageGraphics", ImageSize -> 640, ImageSizeRaw -> {520, 302}, PlotRange -> {{0, 520}, {0, 302}}]], "Output", TextAlignment -> Center, CellMargins -> {{Automatic, Automatic}, {10, 6}}], Cell[TextData[{"Figure 2.3  Spinodal curves for different chain lengths. Longer chains produce wider two-phase regions at higher temperatures (lower \[Chi] threshold). This is why proteins with long disordered regions are prone to phase separation."}], "Text", FontSlant -> Italic, FontSize -> 11, FontColor -> GrayLevel[0.35], CellMargins -> {{70, 70}, {2, 14}}], Cell["3.  Cahn\[Dash]Hilliard dynamics of droplet formation", "Section"], Cell["3.1  The equation", "Subsection"], Cell[TextData[{"The Flory\[Dash]Huggins theory tells us ", StyleBox["whether", FontSlant -> Italic], " phase separation occurs, but not ", StyleBox["how", FontSlant -> Italic], ". The dynamics of a conserved order parameter (composition must be conserved \[LongDash] matter doesn't appear or disappear) are governed by the ", StyleBox["Cahn\[Dash]Hilliard equation", FontWeight -> Bold], ":"}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell[BoxData[FormBox[RowBox[{FractionBox[RowBox[{"\[PartialD]", "\[Phi]"}], RowBox[{"\[PartialD]", "t"}]], "=", RowBox[{"M", " ", SuperscriptBox["\[Del]", "2"], RowBox[{"(", RowBox[{FractionBox[RowBox[{"\[Delta]", "F"}], RowBox[{"\[Delta]", "\[Phi]"}]], "-", RowBox[{"\[Kappa]", " ", SuperscriptBox["\[Del]", "2"], "\[Phi]"}]}], ")"}]}]}], TraditionalForm]], "DisplayFormula", CellMargins -> {{80, 50}, {8, 8}}], Cell[TextData[{"where ", StyleBox["M", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], " is the mobility (diffusion coefficient), ", StyleBox["\[Delta]F/\[Delta]\[Phi]", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], " is the chemical potential from the Flory\[Dash]Huggins free energy, and ", StyleBox["\[Kappa]", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], " is the gradient energy coefficient that penalises sharp interfaces (it sets the surface tension \[Gamma] of the condensate)."}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell[TextData[{StyleBox["Physical content.", FontSlant -> Italic], " The Cahn\[Dash]Hilliard equation says: material flows down chemical potential gradients (as in ordinary diffusion), but the chemical potential now includes a penalty for composition gradients (the \[Kappa] term). Inside the spinodal region, the bulk chemical potential drives ", StyleBox["uphill diffusion", FontWeight -> Bold], " \[LongDash] material flows from dilute to concentrated regions, amplifying any fluctuation. The gradient penalty prevents infinitely sharp interfaces and selects a characteristic wavelength for the initial instability."}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell["3.2  Spinodal decomposition", "Subsection"], Cell[TextData[{"We solve the 1D Cahn\[Dash]Hilliard equation using Wolfram's built-in ", StyleBox["NDSolve", FontFamily -> "Courier"], " with the Method of Lines. The initial condition is the mean composition ", StyleBox["\[Phi] = 0.3", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], " plus small sinusoidal perturbations."}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell["sol = NDSolve[{\n   D[\[Phi][x, t], t] == mob D[\n      mu[\[Phi][x, t]] - \[Kappa] D[\[Phi][x, t], x, x], x, x],\n   \[Phi][x, 0] == \[Phi]0 + noise Sin[2\[Pi] k x / L],\n   \[Phi][0, t] == \[Phi][L, t],  (* periodic BCs *)\n   \[Phi]'[0, t] == \[Phi]'[L, t]},\n   \[Phi], {x, 0, L}, {t, 0, 50},\n   Method -> {\"MethodOfLines\",\n     \"SpatialDiscretization\" -> {\"TensorProductGrid\",\n       \"MinPoints\" -> 200}}];", "Program", CellMargins -> {{60, 30}, {6, 6}}, FontFamily -> "Courier", FontSize -> 11, ShowStringCharacters -> False, Background -> RGBColor[0.95, 0.96, 0.98]], Cell[BoxData[GraphicsBox[TagBox[RasterBox[CompressedData["1:"], {{0, 0}, {520, 526}}, {0, 255}, ColorFunction -> RGBColor],BoxForm`ImageTag["Byte", ColorSpace -> "RGB", Interleaving -> True], Selectable -> False], DefaultBaseStyle -> "ImageGraphics", ImageSize -> 640, ImageSizeRaw -> {520, 526}, PlotRange -> {{0, 520}, {0, 526}}]], "Output", TextAlignment -> Center, CellMargins -> {{Automatic, Automatic}, {10, 6}}], Cell[TextData[{"Figure 3.1  Top: space\[Dash]time density plot of the 1D spinodal decomposition. The initially uniform composition rapidly develops concentration fluctuations that coarsen into well-separated dense (red) and dilute (blue) domains. Bottom: composition profiles at selected times showing the progression from small perturbation to fully phase-separated condensates."}], "Text", FontSlant -> Italic, FontSize -> 11, FontColor -> GrayLevel[0.35], CellMargins -> {{70, 70}, {2, 14}}], Cell["3.3  Coarsening: Lifshitz\[Dash]Slyozov\[Dash]Wagner", "Subsection"], Cell[TextData[{"Once phase separation is complete, the system continues to evolve: large droplets grow at the expense of small ones. This is ", StyleBox["Ostwald ripening", FontWeight -> Bold], ", described by the Lifshitz\[Dash]Slyozov\[Dash]Wagner (LSW) theory. The critical radius ", StyleBox["R*", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], " is set by the Gibbs\[Dash]Thomson equation:"}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell[BoxData[FormBox[RowBox[{SubscriptBox["c", "eq"], "(", "R", ")", "=", SubscriptBox["c", "\[Infinity]"], RowBox[{"exp", "(", FractionBox[RowBox[{"2", "\[Gamma]", " ", SubscriptBox["V", "m"]}], RowBox[{"R", " ", "k", "T"}]], ")"}], "\[TildeTilde]", SubscriptBox["c", "\[Infinity]"], RowBox[{"(", RowBox[{"1", "+", FractionBox[SubscriptBox["l", "c"], "R"]}], ")"}]}], TraditionalForm]], "DisplayFormula", CellMargins -> {{80, 50}, {8, 8}}], Cell[TextData[{"Droplets with ", StyleBox["R < R*", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], " have higher equilibrium concentration than the bulk \[LongDash] they dissolve. Droplets with ", StyleBox["R > R*", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], " have lower equilibrium concentration \[LongDash] they grow. The mean domain size grows as ", StyleBox["\[LeftAngleBracket]R\[RightAngleBracket] \[Proportional] t^(1/3)", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], ", the classic LSW scaling."}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell[BoxData[GraphicsBox[TagBox[RasterBox[CompressedData["1:"], {{0, 0}, {520, 167}}, {0, 255}, ColorFunction -> RGBColor],BoxForm`ImageTag["Byte", ColorSpace -> "RGB", Interleaving -> True], Selectable -> False], DefaultBaseStyle -> "ImageGraphics", ImageSize -> 640, ImageSizeRaw -> {520, 167}, PlotRange -> {{0, 520}, {0, 167}}]], "Output", TextAlignment -> Center, CellMargins -> {{Automatic, Automatic}, {10, 6}}], Cell[TextData[{"Figure 3.2  Ostwald ripening of a polydisperse condensate population. Upper left: size distribution evolution (early: broad, late: narrower with larger mean). Upper right: number of surviving droplets decreases as small ones dissolve. Lower left: mean and max radius vs time with t^(1/3) fit. Lower right: schematic showing material transfer from small to large droplets."}], "Text", FontSlant -> Italic, FontSize -> 11, FontColor -> GrayLevel[0.35], CellMargins -> {{70, 70}, {2, 14}}], Cell["4.  Concentration enhancement and catalysis", "Section"], Cell["4.1  The partition coefficient", "Subsection"], Cell[TextData[{"When a condensate forms, molecules partition between the dense and dilute phases according to their chemical affinity. The ", StyleBox["partition coefficient", FontWeight -> Bold], " ", StyleBox["K", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], " is defined as:"}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell[BoxData[FormBox[RowBox[{"K", "=", FractionBox[SubscriptBox["c", "in"], SubscriptBox["c", "out"]]}], TraditionalForm]], "DisplayFormula", CellMargins -> {{80, 50}, {8, 8}}], Cell[TextData[{"For condensate-forming proteins, K is typically 10\[Dash]100. For RNA molecules that interact strongly with the condensate scaffold, K can reach 100\[Dash]1000. A molecule at 1 nM bulk concentration with K = 100 is at 100 nM inside the condensate. A molecule at 1 nM with K = 1000 is at 1 \[Mu]M. This is the concentration miracle."}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell["4.2  Bimolecular rate enhancement", "Subsection"], Cell[TextData[{"For a bimolecular reaction A + B \[Rule] C with second-order rate constant ", StyleBox["k", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], ":"}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell[BoxData[FormBox[RowBox[{SubscriptBox["rate", "dilute"], "=", RowBox[{"k", " ", SubscriptBox["[A]", "out"], " ", SubscriptBox["[B]", "out"]}]}], TraditionalForm]], "DisplayFormula", CellMargins -> {{80, 50}, {8, 8}}], Cell[BoxData[FormBox[RowBox[{SubscriptBox["rate", "condensate"], "=", RowBox[{"k", " ", RowBox[{SubscriptBox["K", "A"], " ", SubscriptBox["[A]", "out"]}], " ", RowBox[{SubscriptBox["K", "B"], " ", SubscriptBox["[B]", "out"]}]}], "=", RowBox[{SubscriptBox["K", "A"], " ", SubscriptBox["K", "B"], " ", SubscriptBox["rate", "dilute"]}]}], TraditionalForm]], "DisplayFormula", CellMargins -> {{80, 50}, {8, 8}}], Cell[TextData[{"If both reactants partition equally (", StyleBox["K\:2090 \[TildeTilde] K\:2091 \[TildeTilde] K", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], "), the reaction rate is enhanced by ", StyleBox["K\262", FontWeight -> Bold], ". With K = 100, this is a ", StyleBox["10,000-fold enhancement", FontWeight -> Bold], ". This is the quantitative answer to the concentration problem in prebiotic chemistry: you don't need a concentrated primordial soup if you have phase separation."}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell[BoxData[GraphicsBox[TagBox[RasterBox[CompressedData["1:"], {{0, 0}, {520, 338}}, {0, 255}, ColorFunction -> RGBColor],BoxForm`ImageTag["Byte", ColorSpace -> "RGB", Interleaving -> True], Selectable -> False], DefaultBaseStyle -> "ImageGraphics", ImageSize -> 640, ImageSizeRaw -> {520, 338}, PlotRange -> {{0, 520}, {0, 338}}]], "Output", TextAlignment -> Center, CellMargins -> {{Automatic, Automatic}, {10, 6}}], Cell[TextData[{"Figure 4.1  Reaction rate enhancement inside condensates as a function of the partition coefficient K. Bimolecular reactions (blue) are enhanced by K\262; trimolecular reactions (red) by K\263. At the typical condensate K \[TildeTilde] 100, bimolecular rates are 10\:2074 higher inside the condensate."}], "Text", FontSlant -> Italic, FontSize -> 11, FontColor -> GrayLevel[0.35], CellMargins -> {{70, 70}, {2, 14}}], Cell["4.3  Prebiotic chemistry phase space", "Subsection"], Cell[TextData[{"Which combinations of partition coefficient and bulk concentration allow net chemical production? We map the net replication rate (replication minus degradation) across the K\[Dash]c parameter space:"}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell[BoxData[GraphicsBox[TagBox[RasterBox[CompressedData["1:"], {{0, 0}, {520, 532}}, {0, 255}, ColorFunction -> RGBColor],BoxForm`ImageTag["Byte", ColorSpace -> "RGB", Interleaving -> True], Selectable -> False], DefaultBaseStyle -> "ImageGraphics", ImageSize -> 640, ImageSizeRaw -> {520, 532}, PlotRange -> {{0, 520}, {0, 532}}]], "Output", TextAlignment -> Center, CellMargins -> {{Automatic, Automatic}, {10, 6}}], Cell[TextData[{"Figure 4.2  Net replication rate as a function of partition coefficient K and bulk substrate concentration. The black contour marks the boundary between net growth (blue) and net decay (red). At low bulk concentrations (the likely prebiotic regime), only condensates with K > 50\[Dash]100 support net replication."}], "Text", FontSlant -> Italic, FontSize -> 11, FontColor -> GrayLevel[0.35], CellMargins -> {{70, 70}, {2, 14}}], Cell["5.  RNA world inside condensates", "Section"], Cell["5.1  Ribozyme kinetics", "Subsection"], Cell[TextData[{"The RNA world hypothesis proposes that the first self-replicating molecules were RNA ribozymes \[LongDash] RNA molecules that catalyse their own replication. The kinetics of ribozyme-catalysed replication follow ", StyleBox["Michaelis\[Dash]Menten", FontWeight -> Bold], " saturation:"}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell[BoxData[FormBox[RowBox[{"v", "=", FractionBox[RowBox[{SubscriptBox["V", "max"], " ", "[S]"}], RowBox[{SubscriptBox["K", "m"], "+", "[S]"}]]}], TraditionalForm]], "DisplayFormula", CellMargins -> {{80, 50}, {8, 8}}], Cell[TextData[{"Inside a condensate, both the enzyme (ribozyme) and the substrate (template RNA) are concentrated by the partition coefficient K. The effective ", StyleBox["V\:2098\:2090\:2093", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], " is enhanced by K (more enzyme), and the effective [S] is enhanced by K (more substrate):"}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell[BoxData[FormBox[RowBox[{SubscriptBox["v", "condensate"], "=", FractionBox[RowBox[{"K", " ", SubscriptBox["V", "max"], " ", "K", " ", "[S]"}], RowBox[{SubscriptBox["K", "m"], "+", "K", " ", "[S]"}]]}], TraditionalForm]], "DisplayFormula", CellMargins -> {{80, 50}, {8, 8}}], Cell[TextData[{"At low substrate concentrations (", StyleBox["K [S] \[LessLess] K\:2098", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], "), the rate scales as ", StyleBox["K\262", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], " \[LongDash] the full bimolecular enhancement. At saturation (", StyleBox["K [S] \[GreaterGreater] K\:2098", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], "), the rate scales as K (only the enzyme enhancement matters)."}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell["5.2  Replication vs degradation", "Subsection"], Cell[TextData[{"RNA is chemically unstable. In the prebiotic ocean, hydrolysis degrades RNA on timescales of hours to days. The ", StyleBox["origin-of-life window", FontWeight -> Bold], " exists only when the replication rate exceeds the degradation rate:"}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell[BoxData[FormBox[RowBox[{SubscriptBox["v", "rep"], ">", SubscriptBox["v", "deg"], "   \[DoubleLongLeftRightArrow]   ", "K", ">", SuperscriptBox["K", "*"]}], TraditionalForm]], "DisplayFormula", CellMargins -> {{80, 50}, {8, 8}}], Cell[TextData[{"where ", StyleBox["K*", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], " is the critical partition coefficient. Below K*, degradation wins and no self-replicating system can persist."}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell[BoxData[GraphicsBox[TagBox[RasterBox[CompressedData["1:"], {{0, 0}, {520, 276}}, {0, 255}, ColorFunction -> RGBColor],BoxForm`ImageTag["Byte", ColorSpace -> "RGB", Interleaving -> True], Selectable -> False], DefaultBaseStyle -> "ImageGraphics", ImageSize -> 640, ImageSizeRaw -> {520, 276}, PlotRange -> {{0, 520}, {0, 276}}]], "Output", TextAlignment -> Center, CellMargins -> {{Automatic, Automatic}, {10, 6}}], Cell[TextData[{"Figure 5.1  Left: Ribozyme replication rate as a function of bulk substrate concentration at different partition coefficients K. Right: Replication (blue) vs degradation (red) rate as a function of K at fixed bulk substrate [S] = 1 \[Mu]M. The green-shaded region is the origin-of-life window where replication outpaces degradation."}], "Text", FontSlant -> Italic, FontSize -> 11, FontColor -> GrayLevel[0.35], CellMargins -> {{70, 70}, {2, 14}}], Cell["5.3  The origin-of-life window", "Subsection"], Cell[TextData[{"The critical partition coefficient K* depends on the ribozyme efficiency (V\:2098\:2090\:2093/K\:2098) and the degradation rate constant. For plausible prebiotic parameters (V\:2098\:2090\:2093 \[TildeTilde] 1 \[Mu]M/min, K\:2098 \[TildeTilde] 10 \[Mu]M, k\:1d48\:2091\:2092 \[TildeTilde] 0.05/min), we find K* \[TildeTilde] 20\[Dash]50. Modern coacervate droplets routinely achieve K = 50\[Dash]500, well above this threshold. The concentration-enhancement mechanism is not marginal \[LongDash] it is comfortably within the window that sustains replication."}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell[TextData[{"This is the quantitative version of Oparin's coacervate hypothesis: phase separation provides the thermodynamic free lunch that bootstraps biochemistry from a dilute ocean."}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell["6.  Protocell dynamics", "Section"], Cell["6.1  Growth and division", "Subsection"], Cell[TextData[{"A condensate droplet that sustains net internal replication grows. Material produced inside the droplet increases its volume. When the droplet exceeds a critical radius (set by the balance between surface tension and internal compositional stress), it becomes mechanically unstable and ", StyleBox["divides", FontWeight -> Bold], " into two daughter droplets. This is not cell division in the modern sense \[LongDash] there is no DNA, no cytoskeleton, no division machinery. It is purely a consequence of soft-matter physics: a liquid droplet under internal pressure that exceeds the Laplace pressure will spontaneously fission."}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell[BoxData[GraphicsBox[TagBox[RasterBox[CompressedData["1:"], {{0, 0}, {520, 166}}, {0, 255}, ColorFunction -> RGBColor],BoxForm`ImageTag["Byte", ColorSpace -> "RGB", Interleaving -> True], Selectable -> False], DefaultBaseStyle -> "ImageGraphics", ImageSize -> 640, ImageSizeRaw -> {520, 166}, PlotRange -> {{0, 520}, {0, 166}}]], "Output", TextAlignment -> Center, CellMargins -> {{Automatic, Automatic}, {10, 6}}], Cell[TextData[{"Figure 6.1  Schematic of the protocell life cycle. Small condensates grow by accretion of material from the bulk and by internal replication. When they exceed a critical size, mechanical instability drives division into two daughters. Daughters inherit (with variation) the parent's composition, including its partition coefficient K and replication efficiency. Weaker daughters dissolve; fitter ones survive and grow."}], "Text", FontSlant -> Italic, FontSize -> 11, FontColor -> GrayLevel[0.35], CellMargins -> {{70, 70}, {2, 14}}], Cell["6.2  Proto-Darwinian selection", "Subsection"], Cell[TextData[{"If daughter droplets inherit their parent's chemical composition (approximately), and if that composition affects their fitness (replication rate, stability), then we have the three ingredients of natural selection: ", StyleBox["variation", FontWeight -> Bold], " (daughters differ slightly from each other), ", StyleBox["inheritance", FontWeight -> Bold], " (daughters resemble parents more than random droplets), and ", StyleBox["differential fitness", FontWeight -> Bold], " (some compositions grow faster than others). This is ", StyleBox["proto-Darwinian selection", FontWeight -> Bold], " \[LongDash] natural selection operating on condensate populations before the invention of genetic material."}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell[TextData[{"We simulate this by initialising a population of 50 protocells with random partition coefficients K and replication rates, then letting them grow, divide, compete for resources, and either survive or dissolve:"}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell[BoxData[GraphicsBox[TagBox[RasterBox[CompressedData["1:"], {{0, 0}, {520, 125}}, {0, 255}, ColorFunction -> RGBColor],BoxForm`ImageTag["Byte", ColorSpace -> "RGB", Interleaving -> True], Selectable -> False], DefaultBaseStyle -> "ImageGraphics", ImageSize -> 640, ImageSizeRaw -> {520, 125}, PlotRange -> {{0, 520}, {0, 125}}]], "Output", TextAlignment -> Center, CellMargins -> {{Automatic, Automatic}, {10, 6}}], Cell[TextData[{"Figure 6.2  Proto-Darwinian selection in a simulated protocell population. Left: mean partition coefficient K increases over time as protocells with higher K out-compete those with lower K. Centre: mean replication rate increases. Right: lineage depth (max generation number) grows as successful lineages proliferate. These trends are the signature of adaptive evolution operating on membrane-free liquid droplets."}], "Text", FontSlant -> Italic, FontSize -> 11, FontColor -> GrayLevel[0.35], CellMargins -> {{70, 70}, {2, 14}}], Cell["7.  Condensates and disease", "Section"], Cell["7.1  Liquid to gel to aggregate", "Subsection"], Cell[TextData[{"Functional condensates are ", StyleBox["liquid", FontWeight -> Bold], ": they flow, they exchange material rapidly with the surrounding cytoplasm, and they dissolve when the cell no longer needs them. But this liquidity is not guaranteed. Over time, or in the presence of disease-associated mutations, condensates can undergo a ", StyleBox["maturation", FontWeight -> Bold], " transition: liquid \[Rule] gel \[Rule] solid aggregate. The gel state has reduced dynamics; the solid aggregate is essentially irreversible."}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell[TextData[{"This maturation is the molecular basis of several ", StyleBox["neurodegenerative diseases", FontWeight -> Bold], ". The proteins FUS, TDP-43, and tau all form functional liquid condensates in healthy neurons. Disease mutations (in ALS, FTLD, Alzheimer's) shift the phase boundaries, accelerating the liquid-to-gel-to-aggregate transition. The resulting solid aggregates are the amyloid plaques and inclusion bodies seen in patient brains."}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell[TextData[{"We model this as a three-state kinetic system:"}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell[BoxData[FormBox[RowBox[{"[liquid]", OverscriptBox["\[LongRightArrow]", SubscriptBox["k", "gel"]], "[gel]", OverscriptBox["\[LongRightArrow]", SubscriptBox["k", "agg"]], "[aggregate]"}], TraditionalForm]], "DisplayFormula", CellMargins -> {{80, 50}, {8, 8}}], Cell[BoxData[GraphicsBox[TagBox[RasterBox[CompressedData["1:"], {{0, 0}, {520, 370}}, {0, 255}, ColorFunction -> RGBColor],BoxForm`ImageTag["Byte", ColorSpace -> "RGB", Interleaving -> True], Selectable -> False], DefaultBaseStyle -> "ImageGraphics", ImageSize -> 640, ImageSizeRaw -> {520, 370}, PlotRange -> {{0, 520}, {0, 370}}]], "Output", TextAlignment -> Center, CellMargins -> {{Automatic, Automatic}, {10, 6}}], Cell[TextData[{"Figure 7.1  Three kinetic scenarios for condensate aging. Left: wild-type protein with slow maturation (healthy neuron). Centre: risk variant with moderate rates. Right: disease mutant (FUS/TDP-43 in ALS) with fast maturation \[LongDash] the liquid phase is rapidly depleted, and toxic aggregates accumulate."}], "Text", FontSlant -> Italic, FontSize -> 11, FontColor -> GrayLevel[0.35], CellMargins -> {{70, 70}, {2, 14}}], Cell["7.2  Phase diagram of material states", "Subsection"], Cell[TextData[{"The condensate material state depends on protein concentration and temperature (or interaction strength). We construct a schematic phase diagram showing the four regimes: mixed (one phase), liquid condensate, gel, and solid aggregate. Disease mutations effectively shift the boundaries, expanding the gel and aggregate regions at the expense of the functional liquid state."}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell[BoxData[GraphicsBox[TagBox[RasterBox[CompressedData["1:"], {{0, 0}, {520, 280}}, {0, 255}, ColorFunction -> RGBColor],BoxForm`ImageTag["Byte", ColorSpace -> "RGB", Interleaving -> True], Selectable -> False], DefaultBaseStyle -> "ImageGraphics", ImageSize -> 640, ImageSizeRaw -> {520, 280}, PlotRange -> {{0, 520}, {0, 280}}]], "Output", TextAlignment -> Center, CellMargins -> {{Automatic, Automatic}, {10, 6}}], Cell[TextData[{"Figure 7.2  Condensate material state phase diagram. Higher concentration and lower temperature (stronger interactions) drive the progression from mixed solution to liquid condensate to gel to solid aggregate. Disease mutations shift the boundaries toward lower concentrations and higher temperatures, making pathological aggregation more likely."}], "Text", FontSlant -> Italic, FontSize -> 11, FontColor -> GrayLevel[0.35], CellMargins -> {{70, 70}, {2, 14}}], Cell["8.  Nonlinear dynamics: stability, bifurcation, and coarsening", "Section"], Cell[TextData[{"The previous sections built the model; here we analyse it with the toolkit of ", StyleBox["nonlinear dynamics", FontWeight -> Bold], " \[LongDash] linear stability, bifurcation theory, Lyapunov functionals, and scaling laws. Every figure in this section is generated by ", StyleBox["wolfram/nonlinear_dynamics.wls", FontFamily -> "Courier"], " (with a parallel Python implementation in ", StyleBox["src/nonlinear_analysis.py", FontFamily -> "Courier"], ")."}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell["8.1  Linear stability and the dispersion relation", "Subsection"], Cell[TextData[{"Linearise the Cahn\[Dash]Hilliard equation about a uniform state ", StyleBox["\[Phi] = \[Phi]\:2080 + \[Delta]\[ThinSpace]exp(i\[ThinSpace]k\[CenterDot]x + \[Omega]t)", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], ". Each Fourier mode grows or decays independently at the rate"}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell[BoxData[FormBox[RowBox[{"\[Omega]", "(", "k", ")", "=", RowBox[{"-", "M", " ", SuperscriptBox["k", "2"], " ", RowBox[{"(", RowBox[{SuperscriptBox["f", "\[Prime]\[Prime]"], "(", SubscriptBox["\[Phi]", "0"], ")", "+", "\[Kappa]", " ", SuperscriptBox["k", "2"]}], ")"}]}]}], TraditionalForm]], "DisplayFormula", CellMargins -> {{80, 50}, {8, 8}}], Cell[TextData[{"A uniform state is ", StyleBox["unstable", FontWeight -> Bold], " precisely when ", StyleBox["f\[Prime]\[Prime](\[Phi]\:2080) < 0", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], " \[LongDash] the ", StyleBox["spinodal", FontWeight -> Bold], " condition \[LongDash] in which case modes with ", StyleBox["0 < k < k_c = sqrt(-f\[Prime]\[Prime]/\[Kappa])", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], " grow. The fastest-growing mode and its rate are ", StyleBox["k* = sqrt(-f\[Prime]\[Prime]/2\[Kappa])", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], " and ", StyleBox["\[Omega]* = M (f\[Prime]\[Prime])\262/4\[Kappa]", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], ", setting the initial droplet spacing ", StyleBox["\[Lambda]* = 2\[Pi]/k*", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], ". This single mode is the seed of all condensate morphology."}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell[BoxData[GraphicsBox[TagBox[RasterBox[CompressedData["1:"], {{0, 0}, {520, 338}}, {0, 255}, ColorFunction -> RGBColor],BoxForm`ImageTag["Byte", ColorSpace -> "RGB", Interleaving -> True], Selectable -> False], DefaultBaseStyle -> "ImageGraphics", ImageSize -> 640, ImageSizeRaw -> {520, 338}, PlotRange -> {{0, 520}, {0, 338}}]], "Output", TextAlignment -> Center, CellMargins -> {{Automatic, Automatic}, {10, 6}}], Cell[TextData[{"Figure 8.1  Cahn\[Dash]Hilliard dispersion relation for the Flory\[Dash]Huggins free energy (\[Chi] = 1.5). Curves with a positive lobe are unstable; dots mark the fastest-growing mode k*. As the mean composition \[Phi]\:2080 approaches the spinodal edge, the growth rate collapses to zero and the selected wavelength diverges."}], "Text", FontSlant -> Italic, FontSize -> 11, FontColor -> GrayLevel[0.35], CellMargins -> {{70, 70}, {2, 14}}], Cell["8.2  Mode selection across the parameter plane", "Subsection"], Cell[TextData[{"The selected wavelength ", StyleBox["\[Lambda]* = 2\[Pi] sqrt(2\[Kappa]/(-f\[Prime]\[Prime]))", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], " grows with the surface-tension parameter ", StyleBox["\[Kappa]", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], " and diverges at the spinodal boundary, where ", StyleBox["f\[Prime]\[Prime] \[Rule] 0", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], ". The map below shows how the length scale is selected jointly by composition and ", StyleBox["\[Kappa]", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], "."}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell[BoxData[GraphicsBox[TagBox[RasterBox[CompressedData["1:"], {{0, 0}, {520, 489}}, {0, 255}, ColorFunction -> RGBColor],BoxForm`ImageTag["Byte", ColorSpace -> "RGB", Interleaving -> True], Selectable -> False], DefaultBaseStyle -> "ImageGraphics", ImageSize -> 640, ImageSizeRaw -> {520, 489}, PlotRange -> {{0, 520}, {0, 489}}]], "Output", TextAlignment -> Center, CellMargins -> {{Automatic, Automatic}, {10, 6}}], Cell[TextData[{"Figure 8.2  Fastest-growing wavelength \[Lambda]* over the (\[Phi]\:2080, \[Kappa]) plane (clipped at 25 for contrast). The white curve is the spinodal f\[Prime]\[Prime] = 0; outside it the homogeneous state is linearly stable and no length scale is selected."}], "Text", FontSlant -> Italic, FontSize -> 11, FontColor -> GrayLevel[0.35], CellMargins -> {{70, 70}, {2, 14}}], Cell["8.3  Bifurcation: common-tangent coexistence vs. the spinodal", "Subsection"], Cell[TextData[{"Sweeping the control parameter traces out the ", StyleBox["bifurcation diagram", FontWeight -> Bold], ". For the symmetric double well ", StyleBox["f = -a/2\[ThinSpace]\[Phi]\262 + b/4\[ThinSpace]\[Phi]\:2074", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], " the coexistence amplitude follows a supercritical pitchfork: the binodal ", StyleBox["\[Phi] = \[PlusMinus]sqrt(a/b)", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], " encloses the spinodal ", StyleBox["\[Phi] = \[PlusMinus]sqrt(a/3b)", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], ". For Flory\[Dash]Huggins the coexistence curve is the ", StyleBox["common-tangent", FontWeight -> Bold], " (Maxwell) construction. Because the osmotic pressure is linear in \[Chi], the dense branch follows the closed form ", StyleBox["\[Chi](\[Phi]_R) = (\[Phi]_R g(\[Phi]_R) - h(\[Phi]_R))/\[Phi]_R\262", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], ", which is numerically robust where the repository's earlier two-variable solver failed."}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell[BoxData[GraphicsBox[TagBox[RasterBox[CompressedData["1:"], {{0, 0}, {520, 174}}, {0, 255}, ColorFunction -> RGBColor],BoxForm`ImageTag["Byte", ColorSpace -> "RGB", Interleaving -> True], Selectable -> False], DefaultBaseStyle -> "ImageGraphics", ImageSize -> 640, ImageSizeRaw -> {520, 174}, PlotRange -> {{0, 520}, {0, 174}}]], "Output", TextAlignment -> Center, CellMargins -> {{Automatic, Automatic}, {10, 6}}], Cell[TextData[{"Figure 8.3  (a) Flory\[Dash]Huggins phase diagram: binodal (coexistence, blue) outside the spinodal (instability, red dashed), meeting at the critical point; the orange dot marks the solver operating point (\[Phi] = 0.3, \[Chi] = 1.5). (b) The symmetric double-well pitchfork, with the metastable region between binodal and spinodal."}], "Text", FontSlant -> Italic, FontSize -> 11, FontColor -> GrayLevel[0.35], CellMargins -> {{70, 70}, {2, 14}}], Cell["8.4  The free energy as a Lyapunov functional", "Subsection"], Cell[TextData[{"Cahn\[Dash]Hilliard dynamics are a ", StyleBox["gradient flow", FontWeight -> Bold], " of the free-energy functional ", StyleBox["F[\[Phi]] = \[Integral] [ f(\[Phi]) + (\[Kappa]/2) |\[Del]\[Phi]|\262 ] dx", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], ". Conservation of \[Phi] and the structure of the equation guarantee ", StyleBox["dF/dt \[LessEqual] 0", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], ": F is a ", StyleBox["Lyapunov functional", FontWeight -> Bold], " and the phase-separated state is its minimiser. Each coarsening event \[LongDash] two domains merging \[LongDash] appears as a step down in F."}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell[BoxData[GraphicsBox[TagBox[RasterBox[CompressedData["1:"], {{0, 0}, {520, 184}}, {0, 255}, ColorFunction -> RGBColor],BoxForm`ImageTag["Byte", ColorSpace -> "RGB", Interleaving -> True], Selectable -> False], DefaultBaseStyle -> "ImageGraphics", ImageSize -> 640, ImageSizeRaw -> {520, 184}, PlotRange -> {{0, 520}, {0, 184}}]], "Output", TextAlignment -> Center, CellMargins -> {{Automatic, Automatic}, {10, 6}}], Cell[TextData[{"Figure 8.4  (a) Space\[Dash]time evolution of the 1D double-well Cahn\[Dash]Hilliard equation (NDSolve): domains form, then coarsen by merging. (b) The free energy F[\[Phi]] decreases monotonically, falling in discrete steps as domains coalesce \[LongDash] the signature of a Lyapunov functional. (Note: the strongly stabilised Flory\[Dash]Huggins solver instead freezes; the canonical t\[ThinSpace]^(1/3) coarsening, with ", StyleBox["L\263 = L\:2080\263 + K\[ThinSpace]t", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], " linear in time, is reproduced by the double-well Model B in the Python implementation.)"}], "Text", FontSlant -> Italic, FontSize -> 11, FontColor -> GrayLevel[0.35], CellMargins -> {{70, 70}, {2, 14}}], Cell["8.5  Mass-conserving reaction enhancement", "Subsection"], Cell[TextData[{"Section 4 estimated the bimolecular rate boost inside a condensate as ", StyleBox["K_A K_B", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], ". That is the ", StyleBox["open-system", FontSlant -> Italic], " limit (buffered reservoir). Imposing ", StyleBox["mass conservation", FontWeight -> Bold], " \[LongDash] the condensate concentrates reactants but ", StyleBox["depletes", FontSlant -> Italic], " the surrounding dilute phase \[LongDash] gives a very different answer. With dense area fraction ", StyleBox["p", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], " and rapid partitioning ", StyleBox["c_in = K\[ThinSpace]c_out", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], " in each phase, the volume-averaged rate relative to a well-mixed system is"}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell[BoxData[FormBox[RowBox[{FractionBox[SubscriptBox["k", "eff"], SubscriptBox["k", "out"]], "=", FractionBox[RowBox[{"p", " ", SubscriptBox["K", "A"], SubscriptBox["K", "B"], "+", RowBox[{"(", RowBox[{"1", "-", "p"}], ")"}]}], RowBox[{RowBox[{"(", RowBox[{"1", "+", RowBox[{"p", RowBox[{"(", RowBox[{SubscriptBox["K", "A"], "-", "1"}], ")"}]}]}], ")"}], RowBox[{"(", RowBox[{"1", "+", RowBox[{"p", RowBox[{"(", RowBox[{SubscriptBox["K", "B"], "-", "1"}], ")"}]}]}], ")"}]}]]}], TraditionalForm]], "DisplayFormula", CellMargins -> {{80, 50}, {8, 8}}], Cell[TextData[{"The enhancement vanishes at both ", StyleBox["p \[Rule] 0", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], " (no condensate) and ", StyleBox["p \[Rule] 1", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], " (everything uniform); it is maximal at an ", StyleBox["intermediate dense fraction", FontWeight -> Bold], " ", StyleBox["p* \[TildeTilde] 1/K", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], ", where the gain is only ", StyleBox["\[TildeTilde] K/4", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], " \[LongDash] not the naive ", StyleBox["K\262", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], ". Bulk depletion is the hard ceiling on condensate catalysis, and it predicts an optimal droplet volume fraction \[LongDash] a falsifiable, quantitative claim."}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell[BoxData[GraphicsBox[TagBox[RasterBox[CompressedData["1:"], {{0, 0}, {520, 259}}, {0, 255}, ColorFunction -> RGBColor],BoxForm`ImageTag["Byte", ColorSpace -> "RGB", Interleaving -> True], Selectable -> False], DefaultBaseStyle -> "ImageGraphics", ImageSize -> 640, ImageSizeRaw -> {520, 259}, PlotRange -> {{0, 520}, {0, 259}}]], "Output", TextAlignment -> Center, CellMargins -> {{Automatic, Automatic}, {10, 6}}], Cell[TextData[{"Figure 8.5  Mass-conserving rate enhancement ", StyleBox["k_eff/k_out", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], ". (a) Dependence on the partition coefficients at fixed dense fraction p = 0.05. (b) Dependence on dense fraction for several K: each curve peaks at p* \[TildeTilde] 1/K with a maximum gain of order K/4, far below the open-system K\262."}], "Text", FontSlant -> Italic, FontSize -> 11, FontColor -> GrayLevel[0.35], CellMargins -> {{70, 70}, {2, 14}}], Cell["9.  Active and chemically-maintained condensates", "Section"], Cell[TextData[{"Living condensates are not at equilibrium: ATP-driven reactions continually build up and break down their components. This section extends the model with chemistry. Code: ", StyleBox["src/active_models.py", FontFamily -> "Courier"], ", ", StyleBox["src/fh_extensions.py", FontFamily -> "Courier"], ", and ", StyleBox["wolfram/active_dynamics.wls", FontFamily -> "Courier"], "."}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell["9.1  Reaction-arrested coarsening", "Subsection"], Cell[TextData[{"Add a linear chemical reaction that interconverts droplet material and a soluble form, ", StyleBox["s(\[Phi]) = k_+(1-\[Phi]) - k_-\[Phi] = -k_react(\[Phi]-\[Phi]_ss)", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], ", to the conserved dynamics:"}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell[BoxData[FormBox[RowBox[{FractionBox["\[PartialD]\[Phi]", "\[PartialD]t"], "=", "M", " ", SuperscriptBox["\[Del]", "2"], "\[Mu]", "-", SubscriptBox["k", "react"], RowBox[{"(", RowBox[{"\[Phi]", "-", SubscriptBox["\[Phi]", "ss"]}], ")"}]}], TraditionalForm]], "DisplayFormula", CellMargins -> {{80, 50}, {8, 8}}], Cell[TextData[{"The reaction adds a uniform ", StyleBox["-k_react", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], " to the growth rate, ", StyleBox["\[Omega](k) = -Mk\262(f\[Prime]\[Prime]+\[Kappa]k\262) - k_react", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], ", which ", StyleBox["stabilises the long-wavelength modes", FontWeight -> Bold], ": the unstable band becomes finite and a length scale is selected. Ostwald ripening is arrested and the system settles into a steady-state emulsion \[LongDash] the mechanism behind chemically-active droplets (Zwicker 2015; Bauermann 2024)."}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell[BoxData[GraphicsBox[TagBox[RasterBox[CompressedData["1:"], {{0, 0}, {520, 341}}, {0, 255}, ColorFunction -> RGBColor],BoxForm`ImageTag["Byte", ColorSpace -> "RGB", Interleaving -> True], Selectable -> False], DefaultBaseStyle -> "ImageGraphics", ImageSize -> 640, ImageSizeRaw -> {520, 341}, PlotRange -> {{0, 520}, {0, 341}}]], "Output", TextAlignment -> Center, CellMargins -> {{Automatic, Automatic}, {10, 6}}], Cell[TextData[{"Figure 9.1  Reaction-modified dispersion relation. Increasing the reaction rate pushes the small-k (long-wavelength) modes below zero, leaving a finite unstable band -- coarsening can no longer proceed to arbitrarily large domains."}], "Text", FontSlant -> Italic, FontSize -> 11, FontColor -> GrayLevel[0.35], CellMargins -> {{70, 70}, {2, 14}}], Cell[BoxData[GraphicsBox[TagBox[RasterBox[CompressedData["1:"], {{0, 0}, {520, 266}}, {0, 255}, ColorFunction -> RGBColor],BoxForm`ImageTag["Byte", ColorSpace -> "RGB", Interleaving -> True], Selectable -> False], DefaultBaseStyle -> "ImageGraphics", ImageSize -> 640, ImageSizeRaw -> {520, 266}, PlotRange -> {{0, 520}, {0, 266}}]], "Output", TextAlignment -> Center, CellMargins -> {{Automatic, Automatic}, {10, 6}}], Cell[TextData[{"Figure 9.2  1D reaction-Cahn-Hilliard (NDSolve). (a) Without reaction the domains coarsen. (b) With reaction the pattern freezes into a steady-state periodic structure of selected wavelength."}], "Text", FontSlant -> Italic, FontSize -> 11, FontColor -> GrayLevel[0.35], CellMargins -> {{70, 70}, {2, 14}}], Cell[TextData[{"In 2D (Python spectral solver) the same mechanism turns the coarse, ever-growing morphology into a fine ", StyleBox["steady-state emulsion", FontWeight -> Bold], " whose droplet size ", StyleBox["L*", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], " decreases with reaction rate (measured exponent ", StyleBox["L* ~ k^(-0.20)", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], " in this Brazovskii-type microphase regime, distinct from the droplet reaction-limited ", StyleBox["R* ~ sqrt(D/k)", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], "), and the steady-state size distribution is narrower than the passive LSW one."}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell[BoxData[GraphicsBox[TagBox[RasterBox[CompressedData["1:"], {{0, 0}, {520, 172}}, {0, 255}, ColorFunction -> RGBColor],BoxForm`ImageTag["Byte", ColorSpace -> "RGB", Interleaving -> True], Selectable -> False], DefaultBaseStyle -> "ImageGraphics", ImageSize -> 640, ImageSizeRaw -> {520, 172}, PlotRange -> {{0, 520}, {0, 172}}]], "Output", TextAlignment -> Center, CellMargins -> {{Automatic, Automatic}, {10, 6}}], Cell[TextData[{"Figure 9.3  (Python 2D spectral solver.) Passive coarsening (left) vs. the reaction-arrested steady-state emulsion (centre); the active size distribution (right) is narrower than the passive one (Bauermann 2024)."}], "Text", FontSlant -> Italic, FontSize -> 11, FontColor -> GrayLevel[0.35], CellMargins -> {{70, 70}, {2, 14}}], Cell["9.2  Active Model B: broken detailed balance", "Subsection"], Cell[TextData[{"A genuinely non-equilibrium term ", StyleBox["\[Lambda]|\[Del]\[Phi]|\262", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], " added to the chemical potential breaks detailed balance (Active Model B; Tjhung 2018). Measured honestly, this term shifts the coexistence densities ", StyleBox["asymmetrically", FontWeight -> Bold], " (the dense phase becomes denser than the dilute phase is dilute) and modestly slows coarsening, but does ", StyleBox["not", FontSlant -> Italic], " by itself arrest it \[LongDash] the robust route to a steady-state emulsion is the chemical reaction of \[Section]9.1."}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell["9.3  Stickers and spacers: multivalency drives phase separation", "Subsection"], Cell[TextData[{"Real biomolecules interact through specific, saturable contacts (\"stickers\" joined by flexible \"spacers\"; Harmon & Pappu 2017). Adding a saturable association free energy ", StyleBox["f_bond = \[Phi]_s [ln(1-p)+p/2]", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], " (bond fraction ", StyleBox["p", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], " from the law of mass action ", StyleBox["p/(1-p)\262 = K_a \[Phi]_s", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], ", ", StyleBox["\[Phi]_s = v\[Phi]", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], ") to the Flory-Huggins free energy expands the two-phase region and lowers the critical interaction: higher sticker valence ", StyleBox["v", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], " makes phase separation far easier."}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell[BoxData[GraphicsBox[TagBox[RasterBox[CompressedData["1:"], {{0, 0}, {520, 340}}, {0, 255}, ColorFunction -> RGBColor],BoxForm`ImageTag["Byte", ColorSpace -> "RGB", Interleaving -> True], Selectable -> False], DefaultBaseStyle -> "ImageGraphics", ImageSize -> 640, ImageSizeRaw -> {520, 340}, PlotRange -> {{0, 520}, {0, 340}}]], "Output", TextAlignment -> Center, CellMargins -> {{Automatic, Automatic}, {10, 6}}], Cell[TextData[{"Figure 9.4  Stickers-and-spacers spinodal curves. Each curve bounds the two-phase region; increasing sticker valence pushes the critical point down and outward -- multivalency drives liquid-liquid phase separation even when simple mixing is unfavourable."}], "Text", FontSlant -> Italic, FontSize -> 11, FontColor -> GrayLevel[0.35], CellMargins -> {{70, 70}, {2, 14}}], Cell["9.4  Thermal-gradient fission: membrane-free division", "Subsection"], Cell[TextData[{"A temperature gradient makes the interaction ", StyleBox["\[Chi] ~ 1/T", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], " position-dependent. A warm band (weakened cohesion) drawn through an elongated droplet dissolves its neck and splits it into two daughters \[LongDash] division without a membrane, a candidate protocell mechanism (Ianeselli 2022). The split is genuinely band-driven: two domains form with the band, only one without it."}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell[BoxData[GraphicsBox[TagBox[RasterBox[CompressedData["1:"], {{0, 0}, {520, 120}}, {0, 255}, ColorFunction -> RGBColor],BoxForm`ImageTag["Byte", ColorSpace -> "RGB", Interleaving -> True], Selectable -> False], DefaultBaseStyle -> "ImageGraphics", ImageSize -> 640, ImageSizeRaw -> {520, 120}, PlotRange -> {{0, 520}, {0, 120}}]], "Output", TextAlignment -> Center, CellMargins -> {{Automatic, Automatic}, {10, 6}}], Cell[TextData[{"Figure 9.5  (Python 2D spectral solver.) An elongated droplet (left) is divided by a central warm band where the well depth a(x) dips below zero (right): the neck dissolves and two daughter droplets pinch off, conserving mass."}], "Text", FontSlant -> Italic, FontSize -> 11, FontColor -> GrayLevel[0.35], CellMargins -> {{70, 70}, {2, 14}}], Cell["9.5  The dispersion surface", "Subsection"], Cell[TextData[{"Finally, the linear-stability result of \[Section]8 viewed as a surface: the growth rate ", StyleBox["\[Omega](k,\[Phi]_0)", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], " over the whole plane of wavenumber and composition, with the neutral-stability ridge ", StyleBox["\[Omega]=0", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], " marking the boundary of the unstable region."}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell[BoxData[GraphicsBox[TagBox[RasterBox[CompressedData["1:"], {{0, 0}, {520, 464}}, {0, 255}, ColorFunction -> RGBColor],BoxForm`ImageTag["Byte", ColorSpace -> "RGB", Interleaving -> True], Selectable -> False], DefaultBaseStyle -> "ImageGraphics", ImageSize -> 640, ImageSizeRaw -> {520, 464}, PlotRange -> {{0, 520}, {0, 464}}]], "Output", TextAlignment -> Center, CellMargins -> {{Automatic, Automatic}, {10, 6}}], Cell[TextData[{"Figure 9.6  The Cahn-Hilliard dispersion surface \[Omega](k, \[Phi]\:2080) for the Flory-Huggins free energy. The unstable dome shrinks to nothing as the composition approaches the spinodal edge; the cyan curve is the neutral-stability ridge."}], "Text", FontSlant -> Italic, FontSize -> 11, FontColor -> GrayLevel[0.35], CellMargins -> {{70, 70}, {2, 14}}], Cell["10.  Emergent complexity: selection, patterns and cycles", "Section"], Cell[TextData[{"The final layer of the model lets genuinely life-like behaviour emerge from the same physics. Code: ", StyleBox["src/multicomponent.py", FontFamily -> "Courier"], ", ", StyleBox["src/darwinian_selection.py", FontFamily -> "Courier"], ", ", StyleBox["src/reaction_diffusion_cargo.py", FontFamily -> "Courier"], ", ", StyleBox["src/wet_dry_cycling.py", FontFamily -> "Courier"], ", ", StyleBox["src/parameter_space.py", FontFamily -> "Courier"], ", and the Wolfram companion ", StyleBox["wolfram/phase3_extensions.wls", FontFamily -> "Courier"], "."}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell["10.1  Multi-component condensates", "Subsection"], Cell[TextData[{"Real condensates contain dozens of macromolecules. For ", StyleBox["N", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], " mutually attracting components that co-condense in near-stoichiometric proportions, the demixing entropy is reduced as if they were one effective polymer of length ", StyleBox["N", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], ", and the symmetric Flory-Huggins critical point migrates (Qian & Knowles 2022):"}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell[BoxData[FormBox[RowBox[{SubscriptBox["\[Phi]", "c"], "=", FractionBox["1", RowBox[{"1", "+", SqrtBox["N"]}]], ",", "\[InvisibleSpace]", "\[InvisibleSpace]", SubscriptBox["\[Chi]", "c"], "=", FractionBox[RowBox[{"2", " ", SuperscriptBox[RowBox[{"(", "1", "+", SqrtBox["N"], ")"}], "2"]}], "N"]}], TraditionalForm]], "DisplayFormula", CellMargins -> {{80, 50}, {8, 8}}], Cell[TextData[{"As ", StyleBox["N", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], " grows the critical concentration falls toward zero \[LongDash] multivalent mixtures phase-separate at vanishing concentration \[LongDash] and the critical interaction drops, so they demix far more easily. Distinct scaffolds give distinct condensates, and each client partitions selectively into the one it binds best (the physical basis of organelle identity)."}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell[BoxData[GraphicsBox[TagBox[RasterBox[CompressedData["1:"], {{0, 0}, {520, 303}}, {0, 255}, ColorFunction -> RGBColor],BoxForm`ImageTag["Byte", ColorSpace -> "RGB", Interleaving -> True], Selectable -> False], DefaultBaseStyle -> "ImageGraphics", ImageSize -> 640, ImageSizeRaw -> {520, 303}, PlotRange -> {{0, 520}, {0, 303}}]], "Output", TextAlignment -> Center, CellMargins -> {{Automatic, Automatic}, {10, 6}}], Cell[TextData[{"Figure 10.1  Multi-component spinodals for ", StyleBox["N=2,3,5,10", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], " in the Qian\[Dash]Knowles convention; the critical point (filled circles) migrates as ", StyleBox["\[Phi]_c=1/(1+\[Sqrt]N)", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], " toward low concentration and low interaction as the number of components grows."}], "Text", FontSlant -> Italic, FontSize -> 11, FontColor -> GrayLevel[0.35], CellMargins -> {{70, 70}, {2, 14}}], Cell[BoxData[GraphicsBox[TagBox[RasterBox[CompressedData["1:"], {{0, 0}, {520, 173}}, {0, 255}, ColorFunction -> RGBColor],BoxForm`ImageTag["Byte", ColorSpace -> "RGB", Interleaving -> True], Selectable -> False], DefaultBaseStyle -> "ImageGraphics", ImageSize -> 640, ImageSizeRaw -> {520, 173}, PlotRange -> {{0, 520}, {0, 173}}]], "Output", TextAlignment -> Center, CellMargins -> {{Automatic, Automatic}, {10, 6}}], Cell[TextData[{"Figure 10.2  Selective compartmentalization. (a) Boltzmann partition coefficients send each client to its preferred condensate. (b) A ternary (A, B, solvent) free energy with two immiscible minima. (c) Distinct droplets carry distinct molecular cargo."}], "Text", FontSlant -> Italic, FontSize -> 11, FontColor -> GrayLevel[0.35], CellMargins -> {{70, 70}, {2, 14}}], Cell["10.2  Darwinian selection among protocells", "Subsection"], Cell[TextData[{"The intellectual climax. Three coupled fields \[LongDash] a fuel-driven phase field ", StyleBox["\[Phi]", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], " (with a cargo coupling ", StyleBox["-\[Beta]c", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], " so cargo-rich droplets grow osmotically), a replicator cargo ", StyleBox["c", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], " that partitions into the dense phase and replicates at a rate set by a heritable genotype ", StyleBox["g", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], ", and a shared global nutrient \[LongDash] reproduce the full Darwinian cycle. Because the nutrient is shared and a chemostat dilution removes cargo uniformly, replicators whose growth rate ", StyleBox["r_0 g", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], " falls below the dilution rate are out-competed (competitive exclusion). Periodic thermal pulses (warm stripes, ", StyleBox["\[Chi]\[Tilde]1/T", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], ") divide the large droplets, and lineages are tracked by overlap."}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell[TextData[{StyleBox["The result is natural selection from pure physics.", FontWeight -> Bold], " The cargo-weighted mean fitness rises over generations and saturates at a mutation-selection balance \[LongDash] no biology is hand-coded; selection is an emergent consequence of the dynamics."}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell[BoxData[GraphicsBox[TagBox[RasterBox[CompressedData["1:"], {{0, 0}, {520, 221}}, {0, 255}, ColorFunction -> RGBColor],BoxForm`ImageTag["Byte", ColorSpace -> "RGB", Interleaving -> True], Selectable -> False], DefaultBaseStyle -> "ImageGraphics", ImageSize -> 640, ImageSizeRaw -> {520, 221}, PlotRange -> {{0, 520}, {0, 221}}]], "Output", TextAlignment -> Center, CellMargins -> {{Automatic, Automatic}, {10, 6}}], Cell[TextData[{"Figure 10.3  (a) Population mean fitness rises 1.19\[Rule]1.67 over 14 generations and saturates below ", StyleBox["g_max", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], ". (b) The cargo-weighted genotype distribution shifts toward fitter replicators."}], "Text", FontSlant -> Italic, FontSize -> 11, FontColor -> GrayLevel[0.35], CellMargins -> {{70, 70}, {2, 14}}], Cell[BoxData[GraphicsBox[TagBox[RasterBox[CompressedData["1:"], {{0, 0}, {520, 125}}, {0, 255}, ColorFunction -> RGBColor],BoxForm`ImageTag["Byte", ColorSpace -> "RGB", Interleaving -> True], Selectable -> False], DefaultBaseStyle -> "ImageGraphics", ImageSize -> 640, ImageSizeRaw -> {520, 125}, PlotRange -> {{0, 520}, {0, 125}}]], "Output", TextAlignment -> Center, CellMargins -> {{Automatic, Automatic}, {10, 6}}], Cell[TextData[{"Figure 10.4  Snapshots of the droplet field coloured by fitness (yellow = fitter). Low-fitness (blue) droplets dissolve while fit droplets proliferate by fission and dominate the population."}], "Text", FontSlant -> Italic, FontSize -> 11, FontColor -> GrayLevel[0.35], CellMargins -> {{70, 70}, {2, 14}}], Cell[BoxData[GraphicsBox[TagBox[RasterBox[CompressedData["1:"], {{0, 0}, {520, 280}}, {0, 255}, ColorFunction -> RGBColor],BoxForm`ImageTag["Byte", ColorSpace -> "RGB", Interleaving -> True], Selectable -> False], DefaultBaseStyle -> "ImageGraphics", ImageSize -> 640, ImageSizeRaw -> {520, 280}, PlotRange -> {{0, 520}, {0, 280}}]], "Output", TextAlignment -> Center, CellMargins -> {{Automatic, Automatic}, {10, 6}}], Cell[TextData[{"Figure 10.5  Protocell lineages: a genuine phylogeny in which the fit (yellow) lineage branches and takes over while low-fitness lineages go extinct (node colour = fitness, size = cargo load)."}], "Text", FontSlant -> Italic, FontSize -> 11, FontColor -> GrayLevel[0.35], CellMargins -> {{70, 70}, {2, 14}}], Cell[BoxData[GraphicsBox[TagBox[RasterBox[CompressedData["1:"], {{0, 0}, {520, 177}}, {0, 255}, ColorFunction -> RGBColor],BoxForm`ImageTag["Byte", ColorSpace -> "RGB", Interleaving -> True], Selectable -> False], DefaultBaseStyle -> "ImageGraphics", ImageSize -> 640, ImageSizeRaw -> {520, 177}, PlotRange -> {{0, 520}, {0, 177}}]], "Output", TextAlignment -> Center, CellMargins -> {{Automatic, Automatic}, {10, 6}}], Cell[TextData[{"Figure 10.6  (Wolfram ", StyleBox["NDSolve", FontFamily -> "Courier"], ".) The well-mixed chemostat limit: competing genotypes (right) and the rising mean fitness (left) confirm the spatial result analytically."}], "Text", FontSlant -> Italic, FontSize -> 11, FontColor -> GrayLevel[0.35], CellMargins -> {{70, 70}, {2, 14}}], Cell["10.3  Reaction-diffusion inside condensates", "Subsection"], Cell[TextData[{"A condensate is also a chemical reactor. With substrate ", StyleBox["A", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], " fed from the dilute surroundings, partner ", StyleBox["B", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], " made inside, and the rate enhanced and the diffusivities reduced inside the dense phase, the product forms in a thin shell \[LongDash] a reaction hotspot pinned to the interface. And when crowding slows the activator far more than the inhibitor, the diffusion ratio ", StyleBox["D_v/D_u", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], " crosses the Turing threshold ", StyleBox["only inside", FontSlant -> Italic], " the droplet: spots self-organise inside the condensate and nowhere else."}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell[BoxData[GraphicsBox[TagBox[RasterBox[CompressedData["1:"], {{0, 0}, {520, 142}}, {0, 255}, ColorFunction -> RGBColor],BoxForm`ImageTag["Byte", ColorSpace -> "RGB", Interleaving -> True], Selectable -> False], DefaultBaseStyle -> "ImageGraphics", ImageSize -> 640, ImageSizeRaw -> {520, 142}, PlotRange -> {{0, 520}, {0, 142}}]], "Output", TextAlignment -> Center, CellMargins -> {{Automatic, Automatic}, {10, 6}}], Cell[TextData[{"Figure 10.7  ", StyleBox["A+B\[Rule]P", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], " inside condensates. Confinement (", StyleBox["D_dense/D_dilute=0.08", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], ") keeps substrate ", StyleBox["A", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], " in an outer shell where it meets the interior partner ", StyleBox["B", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], "; the product (rightmost panel) forms in a ring at the interface."}], "Text", FontSlant -> Italic, FontSize -> 11, FontColor -> GrayLevel[0.35], CellMargins -> {{70, 70}, {2, 14}}], Cell[BoxData[GraphicsBox[TagBox[RasterBox[CompressedData["1:"], {{0, 0}, {520, 276}}, {0, 255}, ColorFunction -> RGBColor],BoxForm`ImageTag["Byte", ColorSpace -> "RGB", Interleaving -> True], Selectable -> False], DefaultBaseStyle -> "ImageGraphics", ImageSize -> 640, ImageSizeRaw -> {520, 276}, PlotRange -> {{0, 520}, {0, 276}}]], "Output", TextAlignment -> Center, CellMargins -> {{Automatic, Automatic}, {10, 6}}], Cell[TextData[{"Figure 10.8  Intra-droplet Turing patterns. The activator field forms spots inside the condensates and stays flat in the dilute phase (42\[Times] amplitude contrast); the bar chart shows ", StyleBox["D_v/D_u", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], " crossing the Schnakenberg threshold ", StyleBox["d_c=10.7", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], " inside."}], "Text", FontSlant -> Italic, FontSize -> 11, FontColor -> GrayLevel[0.35], CellMargins -> {{70, 70}, {2, 14}}], Cell[BoxData[GraphicsBox[TagBox[RasterBox[CompressedData["1:"], {{0, 0}, {520, 338}}, {0, 255}, ColorFunction -> RGBColor],BoxForm`ImageTag["Byte", ColorSpace -> "RGB", Interleaving -> True], Selectable -> False], DefaultBaseStyle -> "ImageGraphics", ImageSize -> 640, ImageSizeRaw -> {520, 338}, PlotRange -> {{0, 520}, {0, 338}}]], "Output", TextAlignment -> Center, CellMargins -> {{Automatic, Automatic}, {10, 6}}], Cell[TextData[{"Figure 10.9  (Wolfram.) Schnakenberg linear-stability growth rate: a band of wavenumbers is unstable inside the dense phase while every mode is stable in the dilute phase."}], "Text", FontSlant -> Italic, FontSize -> 11, FontColor -> GrayLevel[0.35], CellMargins -> {{70, 70}, {2, 14}}], Cell["10.4  Wet-dry cycling", "Subsection"], Cell[TextData[{"On the early Earth, rock pools cycled between flooded (wet) and evaporated (dry) states. Cycling the interaction ", StyleBox["\[Chi](t)=\[Chi]_0+\[CapitalDelta]\[Chi] sin(2\[Pi]t/T)", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], " swings the system between a one-phase well (dissolve, remix) and a spinodally unstable double well (re-nucleate many droplets). Each dissolution erases the size differences that drive Ostwald ripening, so a ", StyleBox["population", FontWeight -> Bold], " of droplets is maintained, while a matched constant-", StyleBox["\[Chi]", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], " run coarsens toward a single winner (Ianeselli 2022)."}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell[BoxData[GraphicsBox[TagBox[RasterBox[CompressedData["1:"], {{0, 0}, {520, 331}}, {0, 255}, ColorFunction -> RGBColor],BoxForm`ImageTag["Byte", ColorSpace -> "RGB", Interleaving -> True], Selectable -> False], DefaultBaseStyle -> "ImageGraphics", ImageSize -> 640, ImageSizeRaw -> {520, 331}, PlotRange -> {{0, 520}, {0, 331}}]], "Output", TextAlignment -> Center, CellMargins -> {{Automatic, Automatic}, {10, 6}}], Cell[TextData[{"Figure 10.10  Droplet number over time: under wet-dry cycling the population is repeatedly reset and sustained, whereas constant ", StyleBox["\[Chi]", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], " coarsens away as the Lifshitz\[Dash]Slyozov ", StyleBox["t^(-2/3)", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], " law. Each dry phase also re-concentrates cargo (a prebiotic ratchet)."}], "Text", FontSlant -> Italic, FontSize -> 11, FontColor -> GrayLevel[0.35], CellMargins -> {{70, 70}, {2, 14}}], Cell[BoxData[GraphicsBox[TagBox[RasterBox[CompressedData["1:"], {{0, 0}, {520, 305}}, {0, 255}, ColorFunction -> RGBColor],BoxForm`ImageTag["Byte", ColorSpace -> "RGB", Interleaving -> True], Selectable -> False], DefaultBaseStyle -> "ImageGraphics", ImageSize -> 640, ImageSizeRaw -> {520, 305}, PlotRange -> {{0, 520}, {0, 305}}]], "Output", TextAlignment -> Center, CellMargins -> {{Automatic, Automatic}, {10, 6}}], Cell[TextData[{"Figure 10.11  (Wolfram ", StyleBox["NDSolve", FontFamily -> "Courier"], ".) 1D wet-dry Cahn\[Dash]Hilliard space\[Dash]time: domains dissolve at each wet half-cycle and re-form at each dry half-cycle."}], "Text", FontSlant -> Italic, FontSize -> 11, FontColor -> GrayLevel[0.35], CellMargins -> {{70, 70}, {2, 14}}], Cell["10.5  The accessible parameter space", "Subsection"], Cell[TextData[{"Finally, a map of every regime the model can reach, in four control planes: the thermodynamic stability of the double well with a finite-size cutoff; the active plane of reaction rate versus activity (dissolution / microphase / macrophase); the Flory-Huggins one-phase/two-phase boundary; and the mass-conserving rate-enhancement landscape with its optimal ridge ", StyleBox["p*\[TildeTilde]1/K", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], ". The corners where three regimes meet are the codimension-2 points of the model."}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell[BoxData[GraphicsBox[TagBox[RasterBox[CompressedData["1:"], {{0, 0}, {520, 434}}, {0, 255}, ColorFunction -> RGBColor],BoxForm`ImageTag["Byte", ColorSpace -> "RGB", Interleaving -> True], Selectable -> False], DefaultBaseStyle -> "ImageGraphics", ImageSize -> 640, ImageSizeRaw -> {520, 434}, PlotRange -> {{0, 520}, {0, 434}}]], "Output", TextAlignment -> Center, CellMargins -> {{Automatic, Automatic}, {10, 6}}], Cell[TextData[{"Figure 10.12  The condensate model's parameter space. (A) ", StyleBox["(\[Phi]_0,\[Kappa])", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], ": spinodal / nucleation / homogeneous, with the codim-2 apex where the spinodal meets the finite-size cutoff. (B) ", StyleBox["(k_fuel,\[Lambda])", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], ": dissolution / microphase / macrophase from short 2D simulations. (C) ", StyleBox["(\[Chi],N)", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], ": Flory\[Dash]Huggins two-phase region and critical line. (D) ", StyleBox["(K,p)", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], ": rate enhancement with the optimal ridge."}], "Text", FontSlant -> Italic, FontSize -> 11, FontColor -> GrayLevel[0.35], CellMargins -> {{70, 70}, {2, 14}}], Cell[BoxData[GraphicsBox[TagBox[RasterBox[CompressedData["1:"], {{0, 0}, {520, 268}}, {0, 255}, ColorFunction -> RGBColor],BoxForm`ImageTag["Byte", ColorSpace -> "RGB", Interleaving -> True], Selectable -> False], DefaultBaseStyle -> "ImageGraphics", ImageSize -> 640, ImageSizeRaw -> {520, 268}, PlotRange -> {{0, 520}, {0, 268}}]], "Output", TextAlignment -> Center, CellMargins -> {{Automatic, Automatic}, {10, 6}}], Cell[TextData[{"Figure 10.13  (Wolfram.) The two analytic planes rendered natively: the ", StyleBox["(\[Chi],N)", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], " two-phase region and the ", StyleBox["(K,p)", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], " rate-enhancement landscape with the ", StyleBox["p*\[TildeTilde]1/K", FontFamily -> "Times", FontSlant -> Italic], " ridge."}], "Text", FontSlant -> Italic, FontSize -> 11, FontColor -> GrayLevel[0.35], CellMargins -> {{70, 70}, {2, 14}}], Cell["11.  Conclusions", "Section"], Cell[TextData[{StyleBox["What we showed.", FontSlant -> Italic], " (1) The Flory\[Dash]Huggins lattice theory, implemented in a few lines of Wolfram Language, correctly predicts the qualitative phase behaviour of biomolecular condensates: a UCST phase diagram with the critical concentration shifting to lower values for longer/more multivalent molecules. (2) The Cahn\[Dash]Hilliard equation, solved natively by ", StyleBox["NDSolve", FontFamily -> "Courier"], ", captures the dynamics of spinodal decomposition and the subsequent coarsening (Lifshitz\[Dash]Slyozov t\[ThinSpace]^(1/3) scaling). (3) The concentration enhancement argument (K\262 for bimolecular reactions) provides a quantitative resolution to the dilute-ocean problem in prebiotic chemistry. (4) Protocell simulations show that condensate populations can undergo proto-Darwinian selection without genetic material. (5) The liquid\[Dash]to\[Dash]gel\[Dash]to\[Dash]aggregate transition connects condensate physics to neurodegenerative disease. (6) Layering the model \[LongDash] multi-component mixtures, a replicating cargo with heredity and a shared nutrient, reaction-diffusion, and wet-dry cycling \[LongDash] makes genuinely life-like behaviour emerge: ", StyleBox["natural selection, Turing morphogenesis, and ripening-resistant droplet populations all fall out of the same physics.", FontWeight -> Bold]}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell[TextData[{StyleBox["The bigger picture.", FontSlant -> Italic], " Biomolecular condensates sit at the intersection of soft-matter physics, molecular biology, the origin of life, and neurodegeneration. The mathematical framework \[LongDash] Flory\[Dash]Huggins thermodynamics, Cahn\[Dash]Hilliard dynamics, Michaelis\[Dash]Menten kinetics, Lifshitz\[Dash]Slyozov coarsening \[LongDash] is classical and well-understood. What is new is the recognition that this physics operates inside living cells, that it provides the thermodynamic foundation for protocellular compartments, and that its failure modes are disease. The condensate revolution is not a discovery of new physics; it is the discovery that old physics was hiding in plain sight inside biology."}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell["12.  References", "Section"], Cell[TextData[{StyleBox["Qian, D., Michaels, T. C. T., & Knowles, T. P. J.", FontWeight -> Bold], " (2022). Analytical solution to the Flory\[Dash]Huggins model for multicomponent phase separation. ", StyleBox["Journal of Physical Chemistry Letters", FontSlant -> Italic], " 13, 7853\[Dash]7860. [Multi-component critical point.]"}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell[TextData[{StyleBox["Turing, A. M.", FontWeight -> Bold], " (1952). The chemical basis of morphogenesis. ", StyleBox["Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B", FontSlant -> Italic], " 237, 37\[Dash]72. [Reaction-diffusion patterns.]"}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell[TextData[{StyleBox["Eigen, M.", FontWeight -> Bold], " (1971). Selforganization of matter and the evolution of biological macromolecules. ", StyleBox["Naturwissenschaften", FontSlant -> Italic], " 58, 465\[Dash]523. [Competitive exclusion of replicators.]"}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell[TextData[{StyleBox["Ianeselli, A., Atienza, M., Kudella, P. W., et al.", FontWeight -> Bold], " (2022). Water cycles dissolve and divide protocell-like droplets. ", StyleBox["Nature Chemistry", FontSlant -> Italic], " 14, 32\[Dash]39. [Wet-dry cycling and thermal fission.]"}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell[TextData[{StyleBox["Flory, P. J.", FontWeight -> Bold], " (1942). Thermodynamics of high polymer solutions. ", StyleBox["Journal of Chemical Physics", FontSlant -> Italic], " 10, 51\[Dash]61."}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell[TextData[{StyleBox["Huggins, M. L.", FontWeight -> Bold], " (1942). Some properties of solutions of long-chain compounds. ", StyleBox["Journal of Physical Chemistry", FontSlant -> Italic], " 46, 151\[Dash]158."}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell[TextData[{StyleBox["Cahn, J. W., & Hilliard, J. E.", FontWeight -> Bold], " (1958). Free energy of a nonuniform system. I. Interfacial free energy. ", StyleBox["Journal of Chemical Physics", FontSlant -> Italic], " 28, 258\[Dash]267."}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell[TextData[{StyleBox["Lifshitz, I. M., & Slyozov, V. V.", FontWeight -> Bold], " (1961). The kinetics of precipitation from supersaturated solid solutions. ", StyleBox["Journal of Physics and Chemistry of Solids", FontSlant -> Italic], " 19, 35\[Dash]50."}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell[TextData[{StyleBox["Oparin, A. I.", FontWeight -> Bold], " (1924). ", StyleBox["Proiskhozhdenie Zhizni", FontSlant -> Italic], " (The Origin of Life). Moscow: Moskovskii Rabochii."}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell[TextData[{StyleBox["Brangwynne, C. P., Eckmann, C. R., Courson, D. S., Rybarska, A., Hoege, C., Gharakhani, J., J\[UDoubleDot]licher, F., & Hyman, A. A.", FontWeight -> Bold], " (2009). Germline P granules are liquid droplets that localize by controlled dissolution/condensation. ", StyleBox["Science", FontSlant -> Italic], " 324, 1729\[Dash]1732. [The founding observation.]"}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell[TextData[{StyleBox["Hyman, A. A., Weber, C. A., & J\[UDoubleDot]licher, F.", FontWeight -> Bold], " (2014). Liquid\[Dash]liquid phase separation in biology. ", StyleBox["Annual Review of Cell and Developmental Biology", FontSlant -> Italic], " 30, 39\[Dash]58."}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell[TextData[{StyleBox["Banani, S. F., Lee, H. O., Hyman, A. A., & Rosen, M. K.", FontWeight -> Bold], " (2017). Biomolecular condensates: organisers of cellular biochemistry. ", StyleBox["Nature Reviews Molecular Cell Biology", FontSlant -> Italic], " 18, 285\[Dash]298. [Comprehensive review.]"}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell[TextData[{StyleBox["Shin, Y., & Brangwynne, C. P.", FontWeight -> Bold], " (2017). Liquid phase condensation in cell physiology and disease. ", StyleBox["Science", FontSlant -> Italic], " 357, eaaf4382."}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell[TextData[{StyleBox["Patel, A., Lee, H. O., Jawerth, L., et al.", FontWeight -> Bold], " (2015). A liquid-to-solid phase transition of the ALS protein FUS accelerated by disease mutation. ", StyleBox["Cell", FontSlant -> Italic], " 162, 1066\[Dash]1077. [Disease connection.]"}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell[TextData[{StyleBox["Zwicker, D., Seyboldt, R., Weber, C. A., Hyman, A. A., & J\[UDoubleDot]licher, F.", FontWeight -> Bold], " (2017). Growth and division of active droplets provides a model for protocells. ", StyleBox["Nature Physics", FontSlant -> Italic], " 13, 408\[Dash]413. [Protocell dynamics.]"}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell[TextData[{StyleBox["Dignon, G. L., Zheng, W., Best, R. B., Kim, Y. C., & Mittal, J.", FontWeight -> Bold], " (2018). Relation between single-molecule properties and phase behavior of intrinsically disordered proteins. ", StyleBox["Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences", FontSlant -> Italic], " 115, 9929\[Dash]9934."}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell["13.  Reproducibility", "Section"], Cell[TextData[{"All source code lives in the repository ", StyleBox["github.com/mthiel74/biomolecular-condensates", FontWeight -> "SemiBold"], " (", StyleBox["https://github.com/mthiel74/biomolecular-condensates", FontFamily -> "Courier", FontSize -> 10], ")", ". The pipeline has two parallel tracks: Wolfram Language (analysis scripts in ", StyleBox["wolfram/", FontFamily -> "Courier"], " and this notebook builder in ", StyleBox["community/", FontFamily -> "Courier"], ") and Python (", StyleBox["src/", FontFamily -> "Courier"], ", including a full 2D Cahn\[Dash]Hilliard spectral solver). To regenerate this notebook from scratch:"}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], Cell["# 1. Run all Wolfram analysis scripts (generates docs/images/*.png)\nwolframscript -file wolfram/run_all.wls\n\n# 2. Rebuild this notebook\nwolframscript -file community/build_notebook.wls\n\n# Or, for the Python track:\npip install numpy scipy matplotlib\npython3 src/flory_huggins.py\npython3 src/cahn_hilliard.py\npython3 src/condensate_kinetics.py\npython3 src/protocell.py\npython3 src/nonlinear_analysis.py   # nonlinear-dynamics analysis (\[Section]8)\npython3 src/fh_extensions.py        # stickers-and-spacers (\[Section]9.3)\npython3 src/active_models.py        # active condensates (\[Section]9)\npython3 src/multicomponent.py       # multi-component condensates (\[Section]10.1)\npython3 src/darwinian_selection.py  # Darwinian selection (\[Section]10.2)\npython3 src/reaction_diffusion_cargo.py  # reaction-diffusion (\[Section]10.3)\npython3 src/wet_dry_cycling.py      # wet-dry cycling (\[Section]10.4)\npython3 src/parameter_space.py      # parameter space (\[Section]10.5)\npython3 src/phase3_heroes.py        # hero visualisations", "Program", CellMargins -> {{60, 30}, {6, 6}}, FontFamily -> "Courier", FontSize -> 11, ShowStringCharacters -> False, Background -> RGBColor[0.95, 0.96, 0.98]], Cell[TextData[{Style["Generated on June 20, 2026 with Wolfram Language 15..", FontSlant -> Italic, FontColor -> GrayLevel[0.4]]}], "Text", CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}]}, WindowSize -> {980, 800}, StyleDefinitions -> "Default.nb"]
(* End of Notebook Content *)
